Spiral Safe Veil adapter for AWS Nitro Enclaves
================================================

Status: buildable adapter and migration scaffold, not a production enclave
release. It replaces the archived Nitriding build dependency with
[Veil](https://github.com/Amnesic-Systems/veil), but Veil is not a drop-in
Nitriding binary and the old lifecycle assumptions do not carry over.

What the adapter does
---------------------

The image combines three pieces:

1. Veil's `veil-daemon` runs inside the enclave. It establishes a VSOCK/TUN
   tunnel, creates the attested TLS endpoint, and reverse-proxies HTTPS port
   8443 to Vault's loopback-only HTTP listener.
2. Vault 1.21.4 runs as the unprivileged `vault` user and loads the Spiral Safe
   external plugin binary from `/vault/plugins`.
3. Veil's `veil-proxy` runs as root on the enclave's EC2 parent and supplies
   networking at `10.0.0.1`; the enclave is reachable from that parent at
   `10.0.0.2:8443`.

Veil's public endpoints (`/veil`, `/veil/config`, and `/veil/attestation`) and
the proxied Vault API share the external TLS listener. Veil's internal control
API listens on loopback port 8080. The image signals `/veil/ready` after the
Vault listener starts.

Pinned source and licensing
---------------------------

The build verifies and compiles Veil v0.5.1 at commit
`2b8c06ca651e09b21832f6fc4ae2605371386f76`. The image records the Veil and
Spiral Safe source revisions as OCI labels. It also includes Veil's license at
`/licenses/veil-GPL-3.0.txt` and declares `GPL-3.0-only` in its license label.

Veil is GPL-3.0 licensed. Distributing an image that contains a Veil binary can
create GPL notice and corresponding-source obligations, and combining it with
other software may require additional analysis. Keep the exact source pin,
license file, build instructions, and a durable source offer with every image;
obtain legal review before external distribution. This is engineering guidance,
not a license opinion. The image also carries the Spiral Safe Apache-2.0 license
at `/licenses/spiral-safe-Apache-2.0.txt`. Vault 1.21.4 is distributed under
BUSL-1.1, so a distributor must also review and satisfy HashiCorp's current
Vault license terms. The OCI license label names all three licenses; it is
metadata, not a substitute for notices, source delivery, or legal review.

The Go 1.26 builder and Vault images are pinned by immutable multi-platform
digest. Vault 1.21.4 is a patched 1.x release and the plugin
registration/startup smoke test is part of this migration's verification path.
Continue normal upgrade and security review rather than treating either pin as
permanent.

Build without launching an enclave
-----------------------------------

From this directory:

    VEIL_FQDN=vault.example.com \
      WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=wallet.example.com \
      WEBAUTHN_RP_ORIGINS=https://wallet.example.com \
      make image
    make tools

`make image` creates an ordinary Linux container archive. `make tools` exports
the pinned Linux `veil-proxy` and `veil-verify` binaries into the ignored
`.tools/` directory. Neither target invokes `nitro-cli` or changes enclave
state. The FQDN becomes part of the image and therefore part of its enclave
measurements; use the real immutable deployment name before producing a release
EIF. The WebAuthn relying-party inputs are also measured image inputs and must
match the browser origin that completes each ceremony.

EIF and explicit EC2-host operation
-----------------------------------

EIF creation requires an enclave-enabled EC2 parent with the Nitro Enclaves CLI:

    make eif

Launching is deliberately separate and has no "terminate all" behavior:

    make run

The run script starts the pinned `veil-proxy`, launches one named enclave
without debug mode or an attached console, and then keeps the proxy in the
foreground. It does not terminate the enclave on exit. Host routing, security
groups, a public load balancer, DNS, and access to `10.0.0.2:8443` remain
operator responsibilities. Follow the
[AWS Nitro Enclaves guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/enclaves/latest/user/)
and Veil's source and examples rather than the removed Nitriding host setup.

Unresolved security and lifecycle boundaries
--------------------------------------------

The adapter proves that Veil can wrap the local Vault listener, but it does not
make the overall system production-safe:

* Nitro Enclaves have no persistent storage. `/vault/data` disappears with the
  enclave. The Kubernetes Raft deployment in `../deploy/` is durable, but it
  runs outside the enclave and is a separate topology. An enclave release needs
  a reviewed, attested protocol that encrypts and commits state to durable
  storage outside the enclave.
* Vault starts uninitialized and sealed. There is no attestation-aware bootstrap
  client that verifies Veil, pins its generated TLS key, initializes Vault, and
  delivers recovery material without exposing it to the parent. Standard Vault
  CLI TLS validation alone is not that protocol.
* Plugin registration and enabling require an authorized Vault operator after
  initialization. The image does not contain a root token, fixed checksum, KMS
  credential, or automatic privileged bootstrap.
* Veil provides attestation endpoints and `veil-verify`, but Spiral Safe still
  needs an EC2 integration test that builds the release EIF twice, verifies its
  measurements and application hash, checks nonce-bound attestation, and rejects
  altered images.
* No attestation-bound KMS or workload-identity policy has been implemented for
  this image. Static AWS credentials must never be embedded in an image or EIF.
* The Express API, browser client, authorization layer, and load tests are not
  inside this EIF. Kubernetes and enclave deployment are currently separate
  operational modes.
* `veil-proxy` requires root, `/dev/net/tun`, NAT changes, and VSOCK access on
  the EC2 parent. Those privileges must be isolated and monitored.

Do not call this path production-ready until persistence, bootstrap, attested
identity, KMS policy, disaster recovery, upgrades, and end-to-end transaction
signing are exercised on enclave-enabled EC2 hardware.
